# The project's frame. AppGraphics loads this by address — GraphicsOptions.Compositor names it — and
# the moment it exists the host's built-in DefaultFrame is not used.
#
# Doc 22's virtualized geometry and doc 19's dynamic global illumination are both on here, which is
# the whole point of the sample: every node below is one a document can name, and none of it is
# assembled in C#.
version: 2

resources:
  # SceneColour is declared *and* imported: declared so this document is a whole thing the editor or
  # a test can build against a scratch texture, imported at run time so the pass writes the window.
  # An import wins over a declaration of the same name, which is what makes one document run in both.
  - name: SceneColour
    format: Bgra8UNormSrgb
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, Storage

  # ⚠ What the scene is actually drawn into, and it is not SceneColour. A shading pass writes
  # radiance — values well past one, which is the whole reason a tonemap exists — and SceneColour is
  # the swapchain's eight bits per channel. Drawing HDR straight into it clips every highlight before
  # the curve that was supposed to shape them ever runs, so the tonemap grades an image that has
  # already lost its range.
  - name: SceneHdr
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, Storage

  # CopySource because the screen-probe gather's placement runs on the host: it copies this depth
  # (and SceneNormals below) back every frame — a few frames of ring latency behind, but every
  # frame — and decodes the handful of anchor texels the probes stand on. Without the flag the
  # copy is a validation error per frame — the gather still dispatches, so every counter says
  # it ran while the probes stand on whatever the readback buffer last held.
  - name: SceneDepth
    format: Depth32Float
    usage: DepthStencilTarget, Sampled, CopySource

  # Four 2048² cascades in a 2 × 2 grid, so 4096 × 4096. The size is the node's own arithmetic —
  # ShadowCascades.AtlasSize, whose fold has always been two columns — written out here because a
  # graph resource has to declare its extent and the node has no way to declare one for it.
  #
  # ⚠ This read 8192 × 2048 for a while, under a comment claiming "side by side" was the node's own
  # arithmetic. It never was: the fold is 2 × 2, so cascades 2 and 3 rendered into viewports below
  # the texture's bottom edge — an empty scissor, silently — and the lookup's normalized quadrants
  # read squashed halves of the two tiles that did land. Which wrong texel a fragment read depended
  # on the camera-fitted cascade centres, so the resulting shadows slid and reshaped as the camera
  # turned. The node refuses a mismatched extent by name now; keep this in step with
  # cascadeCount/resolution below.
  - name: ShadowAtlas
    format: Depth32Float
    usage: DepthStencilTarget, Sampled
    width: 4096
    height: 4096

  # And the punctual one: 11 × 11 tiles of 256², so 2816².
  #
  # ⚠ **The shape of that number is the cost of point lights, stated.** This level has eighteen of
  # them and a point light is six tiles — 108 — so an atlas that shadows all of them needs eleven
  # tiles a side whatever the resolution. Sixteen tiles would have shadowed *two* lights and dropped
  # sixteen, which is the fix not working while every counter says it ran.
  #
  # So the resolution is 256 rather than 1024, and that is the trade taken deliberately: at 512 this
  # texture is 127 MB. These lamps reach 22–34 m over geometry made of boxes, where a quarter-size
  # tile costs a slightly softer shadow edge and buys back fifteen sixteenths of the memory. A level
  # whose lamps were **spot** lights — which is what a floodlight on a post physically is — would need
  # eighteen tiles rather than 108 and could afford 1024 each.
  #
  # ⚠ Its extent is the node's arithmetic too — `AtlasSize`, which is `tilesPerSide × resolution` —
  # and there is no more machinery to get it right here than there is above: too small is a scissor
  # rectangle outside the texture. `PunctualShadowRenderer.DroppedLights` counts what did not fit, all
  # of a light's faces at a time, because a point light with four of its six faces rendered leaks
  # along the other two.
  - name: PunctualShadowAtlas
    format: Depth32Float
    usage: DepthStencilTarget, Sampled
    width: 2816
    height: 2816

  # The split's second target: what every surface *is*, kept apart from what lit it. With
  # `ForwardPlus.SplitOutputs` on (see Arena.Paint) the shading pass holds its diffuse ambient back
  # and writes albedo here instead — rgb, with the material's own occlusion in alpha — so the
  # !AmbientCombine at the other end of the frame can multiply real irradiance and real occlusion
  # into it. Rgba16Float rather than eight bits because the visibility resolve writes the same
  # plane through a storage view, and its split path decodes exactly this format.
  - name: SceneAlbedo
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled

  # And the third: world normal in xyz, roughness in alpha — the contract every screen pass below
  # reads, and a zero vector is the combine's sky test. Produced now: the Main pass's third
  # attachment under the same split, where for most of this document's life it was a cleared
  # target the disabled passes would have marched zeroes out of. CopySource on SceneDepth's terms:
  # probe placement reads this plane back too.
  - name: SceneNormals
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, CopySource

  # And the fourth: specular reflectance at normal incidence — the material's f0 — in rgb, alpha
  # unused. Three channels and not one metalness channel, and that is the whole finding: MaterialData
  # keeps a diffuse colour and an f0 rather than a metalness, the pair is underdetermined, and
  # SceneAlbedo above holds base colour times one minus metalness — which is *black* on a metal, so
  # there would be nothing left for a reader to rebuild f0 from. Without it the combine weighed this
  # level's one metal, the ramps, as though it were paint.
  - name: SceneSpecular
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled

  - name: VisibilityBuffer
    format: R32UInt
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, Storage

  # Where every pixel was last frame, as an offset in UV.
  #
  # ⚠ Two *signed* channels with room past one texel of range, which is why it is a float format and
  # not an eight-bit one. A motion vector points either way and a fast pan moves a pixel a long way;
  # an unsigned format would fold half the screen's motion onto the other half, and an eight-bit one
  # would quantise a slow pan into visible steps that a temporal resolve then accumulates.
  - name: SceneMotion
    format: Rg16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled

  # ── the lake ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Doc 35's three planes, and the one declaration in this file that exists purely so a !Copy is
  # legal.
  #
  # ⚠ **SceneCombined is declared here and is produced by !AmbientCombine, which used to declare it
  # itself.** A node's own `DeclareOutput` checks `frame.Has(Output)` first and steps aside, so this
  # wins — and it has to, because a node declares `ColourTarget | Sampled` and nothing else. The water
  # pass reads a *copy* of the frame behind it (§ B1: sampling a target you are writing is undefined,
  # not slow), the copy needs a CopySource on its source, and neither a document nor a node can add a
  # usage to a texture after it is created. The build refuses a !Copy whose ends lack the flags by
  # name, which is the one way this could have failed loudly; it did.
  - name: SceneCombined
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, CopySource, CopyDestination

  - name: SceneCombinedCopy
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: Sampled, CopyDestination

  # The surface plane: the water surface's own device depth in `r`, its coverage in `g`. Doc 35 § D8
  # assumed a G-buffer shading-model id here and there is not one — the deferred path is ⬜ and this
  # engine is Forward+ — so the mesh pass writes the mask an id comparison would have produced.
  #
  # ⚠ Rgba16Float and not Rgba8UNorm, which is what the golden fixtures use. `r` is a *device* depth
  # under this camera's reverse-Z 0.1/1000 planes, where everything past a few metres crowds into the
  # bottom of the range: eight unorm bits put the whole outdoors into about three codes, and the
  # composite then reconstructs a world position that steps. Half-float has its mantissa where
  # reverse-Z has its values.
  - name: WaterSurface
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled

  # And the normal plane: the surface's world normal in `xyz`, its foam in `a`. Signed, so an
  # unsigned format would fold half of every wave onto the other half.
  - name: WaterNormal
    format: Rgba16Float
    usage: ColourTarget, Sampled, CopySource

stages:
  - name: Opaque

  # The second stage every object in the level is also extracted into — see
  # GraphicsOptions.CasterStages, which is what puts the bit in the mask, because a frame document
  # cannot decide what an object is extracted as.
  #
  # `shader: ShadowCaster` is the override: a shadow map records depth, so a caster has no business
  # evaluating a BRDF, and `composeFromMaterial` stays off because ShadowCaster declares no surface
  # slot — composing would split the variant cache once per material for shaders that compile to
  # identical bytes.
  #
  # ⚠ **Back faces, and it used to be front — which put the sun inside closed buildings.** Culling
  # front faces records the *far* side of a caster, which is a fine anti-acne trick for a lone convex
  # object and is wrong the moment a caster is wide and the light is low. This level's sun is six
  # degrees up, so a ray through an 8 m roof slab enters at one edge and leaves at the other eight
  # metres later — and front-culling records the depth of that far edge, so everything the ray passes
  # through in between, which is the whole room, tests as lit. A house with a roof on it had sunlight
  # on the floor and a sun shadow across the inside of its walls.
  #
  # Back-face culling records the nearest surface, which is what a shadow test wants, and hands the
  # acne problem back to the biases — hence the larger numbers. `Lighting.NormalOffset` and
  # `Lighting.ShadowBias` in the shader do the rest.
  #
  # ⚠ If this reintroduces acne — stippled self-shadowing on surfaces facing the sun, worst at
  # grazing angles — `depthBiasSlope` is the one to raise, because the error grows with the angle
  # between the surface and the light. Raising `depthBias` instead detaches contact shadows.
  - name: Shadow
    shader: ShadowCaster
    cull: Back
    # ⚠ **Zero, and it used to be 2.5 and 4.0 — which is the sun coming through a wall.**
    # A raster depth bias is scaled by the polygon's depth *slope* in the shadow map, and this level's
    # sun is seven degrees up: a wall is very nearly edge-on to it, so that slope is enormous and a
    # factor of four pushes the wall's recorded depth right out behind the floor it should be
    # shadowing. The floor then tests as lit, in a band as long as the wall's shadow should have been.
    #
    # They were raised when this stage switched to back-face culling, to buy off the acne that came
    # with it, and at the time the shader's own bias was in normalised depth and effectively useless
    # in the outer cascades. It is metres now — `ShadowMapRenderer.ConstantBias` and `SlopeBias`,
    # applied against each cascade's own depth range — and `Lighting.NormalOffset` moves the sample
    # rather than the comparison. That is the right place for it: a bias that knows the cascade it is
    # in cannot explode on geometry that happens to be edge-on to the light.
    #
    # ⚠ If acne returns, raise `SlopeBias` on the node rather than these. Raising these is what put
    # the sun inside the building.
    depthBias: 0.0
    depthBiasSlope: 0.0
    depthClamp: true

  # The velocity pass, on the same terms as Shadow above: an override shader, no material features,
  # and a second stage every object in the level is also extracted into — see CasterStages in
  # ThirdPersonShooterGame, which is what puts the bit in the mask.
  #
  # ⚠ `depth: TestOnly`. It runs after the Main pass and shares its depth, so a fragment that lost the
  # depth test is a fragment behind something else and has no business writing that pixel's velocity.
  # Writing depth as well would be harmless and pointless; clearing it would put every object back.
  - name: Motion
    shader: MotionVectors
    depth: TestOnly
    sortMode: FrontToBack

  # ── the sparks over the lamps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Filled by extraction like every other stage, just by a different system: the scene's lamps
  # each carry a !VfxEmitter component naming Effects/Embers.vxvfx, VfxExtractionSystem walks
  # them into render objects, and ThirdPersonShooterGame.OnConfigure's ParticleStage is what
  # names this stage as where they land. (An older increment added the render objects by hand
  # from Arena.LightTheLamps — that code and the comment describing it are gone; see
  # ParticleRenderFeature for the drawing half.)
  #
  # ⚠ `blend: Additive`, which is what an ember *is*: light added to whatever is behind it, never
  # light replacing it. `ParticleSprite.rvn` returns straight alpha for exactly this reason — the
  # blend is `src × srcAlpha + dst`, so premultiplying in the shader as well would square the alpha.
  #
  # ⚠ `depth: TestOnly`. A spark is occluded by the wall in front of it and occludes nothing itself:
  # writing depth would make one ember hide the ember behind it, and would put a depth value in
  # SceneDepth that the velocity pass and the AO march would both read as solid geometry.
  #
  # `cull: None` because a billboard's winding depends on which way the camera is facing, and
  # `sortMode: BackToFront` because additive blending does not need sorting for correctness and does
  # need it for the depth test — a near spark drawn first would reject a far one behind it.
  - name: Embers
    blend: Additive
    depth: TestOnly
    cull: None
    sortMode: BackToFront


game: !Sequence
  name: Frame
  children:
    # ── doc 22: the object cull, on the device ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # At the head of the frame, before anything its answer decides for is drawn — the one placement
    # this node has. The group it dispatches through is the host's (ArenaIllumination fills the
    # builder's Visibility slot), because a visibility group holds device memory across frames and
    # a file cannot create one; built with none — the first build, before the game exists — this is
    # a node that does nothing and the frame culls on the CPU exactly as it always did.
    #
    # ⚠ `readBack: false`, and it was true for most of this document's life. True is the safe
    # reading — the work list is exactly what is visible — and its price turned out to be the
    # frame's single hardest stall: the host submitted the dispatch and then sat in a queue-wide
    # WaitIdle *inside* the frame's own build, before the graphics list was even recorded. On
    # MoltenVK the "compute queue" is frequently the same hardware queue as graphics, so the wait
    # drained more than the cull. `indirectDraws` is the other half of the trade: the host's list
    # stays a superset and the twenty-bytes-per-object argument records are what actually remove
    # the device's rejections. `compact` stays off — it needs a count-from-device draw MoltenVK
    # does not have, and without it the padded form is what this asks for anyway.
    #
    # The occlusion half tests against the pyramid the !HiZ node at the foot of this file reduced
    # *last* frame — a cull that runs before anything is drawn can never have anything newer. The
    # price of that one-phase shape is one frame of latency on whatever stops being occluded, which
    # is a shape the artefact note at the foot of this file already knows by name.
    - !GpuCulling
      name: Cull
      readBack: false
      indirectDraws: true

    # ── doc 19 § L1: the clipmap every trace marches ────────────────────────────────────────────
    # The field itself is the host's — see ThirdPersonShooterGame.OnConfigure. With none supplied
    # this node does nothing rather than throwing, which is what lets this file open in an editor
    # that has not started the game.
    - !GlobalDistanceField
      name: Clipmap
      # ⚠ The *qualified* name, and it is not the same string the consumer below writes. A composed
      # slot's bindings are named for the slot — <pass>.<composed type> — so the clipmap writes
      # `DistanceFieldAo.GlobalDistanceField.distanceFieldVolumes[0]` and the pass reading it names
      # the bare type. Getting these the wrong way round refuses to compile, which is the good case;
      # getting one of them merely misspelt is a march that reads nothing and says nothing.
      shader: DistanceFieldAo.GlobalDistanceField

      # And the shading pass — which is no longer where ambient occlusion happens, and still needs
      # this line. `Arena.Paint` keeps the field bound behind ForwardPlus' compose slot (see
      # MarchOcclusion in Arena.cs for both rounds of that story: the room's occlusion moved to the
      # !DistanceFieldAo node below when the split gave its answer a consumer), and it is the
      # *composition* that declares five volumes and a sampler in set 0 — the march permutation
      # only decides whether anything reads them per pixel. Declarations nothing fills are a set
      # written short, and a set written short is every draw in the Main pass refused rather than
      # an effect that is missing.
      passes:
        - ForwardPlus.GlobalDistanceField

    # ── doc 19 § L2: the probes that carry bounced light ────────────────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ `passes` is not a quality setting. The default is IndirectDiffuse alone — the screen pass that
    # needs no material change — and adding ForwardPlus is what hands the field's five volumes and its
    # samplers to the shading pass's set 0. ForwardPlus *declares* those bindings in every variant
    # whatever the permutations say; EffectSetWriter fills a set whole or not at all, so this
    # line missing is not dimmer indirect light, it is every draw in the Main pass refused.
    #
    # **Filled now, and read now — but by the gather below, not by the material.** The filler is
    # the host's — ArenaIllumination wires TracedIrradianceFiller over the same clipmap the node
    # above composites, with the surface cache's bounced radiance behind hits and the Preetham sky
    # behind misses. Who reads it has moved twice, and both moves are the same lesson: for most of
    # this document's life this node ran and *nothing read it* (`UseIrradianceField` was off because
    # nothing filled the field); then the material read it for one increment; now `!ScreenProbeGather`
    # composes it as the far-field its rays terminate into, and `Arena.Paint` turned the in-material
    # read back off — because a field read in the shading pass *and* gathered into the combine's
    # ambient is the same light counted twice. A node running and a node being read are different
    # facts, and this document can only state the first; the second is written where it is decided,
    # in Arena.Paint.
    #
    # ⚠ `budget` is BRICKS — 64 probes each — and four is a *CPU* number. The filler traces on the
    # host, 32 rays a probe through the clipmap, so a frame pays budget × 64 × 32 marches: four
    # bricks is a few milliseconds, and a sweep of the 9 × 3 × 9 grid lands about once a second,
    # which the filler's hysteresis turns into convergence rather than crawl. The device
    # filler is what buys this number back up — CompositorBuilder.IrradianceDeviceFiller — and that
    # is a host change, not a document one.
    - !IrradianceField
      name: Probes
      budget: 4
      dilationPasses: 1
      passes:
        - IndirectDiffuse
        - ForwardPlus

    # ── doc 19 § L4: the cards that remember what surfaces radiate ──────────────────────────────
    # The store, the capture and the two dispatches are the host's — a card atlas outlives any
    # frame — and what this node owns is the order: decode, upload, light, bounce, swap, publish,
    # which is § L4's recurrence and not something a file should be able to invert. Built with no
    # store — the first build — it does nothing, the same answer every field node here gives.
    #
    # The cards were captured at load by the traced reference over the same analytic box fields the
    # clipmap composites (this level is boxes; the closed form has no bake error), and lit once on
    # the CPU so the probe filler above has real bounced radiance behind its hits. Per frame, the
    # two device dispatches keep the *device* planes by the same arithmetic — direct light against
    # the clipmap, then one bounce reading the previous swap — which is the shipping path and what
    # the screen-probe trace below would sample.
    #
    # Its consumer half filled out when the gather turned on: the atlas publishes under the
    # screen-probe trace's slot (the default `passes` list), and that trace runs now — a screen
    # ray that hits geometry reads the card behind the hit, which is § L4 reaching the picture
    # directly. The CPU probe filler still reads it too, through SurfaceCacheRadiance, so the
    # far-field the gather falls back to carries the same bounce.
    - !SurfaceCache
      name: Cache

    # ── the sun's cascades ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Before anything that shades, because the Main pass reads the atlas this fills.
    #
    # `view: Camera` is not optional decoration: with it empty the node fits every cascade to its own
    # fallback camera, which looks down −Z from the origin — shadows correct for a view nobody is
    # looking through and absent from the one that exists. The sun comes from the builder rather than
    # from this file, because the light the frame shades with and the light it casts from have to be
    # one fact.
    #
    # ⚠ **Still here, and deliberately, now that the !VirtualShadow node below the depth exists.**
    # The sun decision is an A/B rather than a swap: the shading pass composes phase 7's lookup
    # (Arena.Paint fills ForwardPlus' directionalShadow slot), and that lookup answers a *sample* —
    # "this much light" or "I have nothing for this point". ClusteredShading.Shadow takes the map's
    # answer where a drawn page has one and falls through to these cascades everywhere else — a page
    # not yet marked, not yet drawn, or past the clipmap's reach — so the sun is shadowed exactly
    # once at every pixel and the frame never has a hole where the map has not caught up. Deleting
    # this node is the end state doc 22 describes; it is owed the traversal casting clusters per
    # page first, because today a virtualized mesh casts through its phase-1 fallback mesh and the
    # cascades are what cover the map's misses.
    - !ShadowMap
      name: Sun
      stage: Shadow
      atlas: ShadowAtlas
      view: Camera
      # ⚠ Four cascades share one texture, so every lookup is `NdcToUv(M · p)` against a matrix
      # with a *tile* folded into it — and which tile a fragment reads is chosen per fragment from
      # its own view depth, which changes as the camera turns. If that fold disagrees with the
      # texture in any way, a fragment reads a neighbouring cascade's depth: real values, from a
      # map fitted to a different centre at a different scale, sliding as the two centres move
      # apart — a shadow rotating with the camera.
      #
      # The cascadeCount: 1 experiment this comment used to narrate ran, and answered: the fold
      # arithmetic was correct on both sides all along, and the sliding came from ShadowAtlas being
      # declared 8192 × 2048 against the node's 2 × 2 fold — see the resource at the top of this
      # file. The node refuses a mismatched extent by name now, so the experiment cannot be needed
      # twice.
      cascadeCount: 4
      resolution: 2048
      shadowDistance: 90.0
      splitLambda: 0.85

    # ── the lamps' shadows ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # The node this document has had all along, now with somebody to give its atlas to.
    #
    # ⚠ **`passes:` is the whole difference between this frame and every frame before it.** Without
    # it the tiles are packed, the casters drawn and the atlas filled, and nothing samples a texel of
    # it — which is what was happening: every spot and point light in the level lit through walls,
    # because the shader half of this feature did not exist. `Raven/Library/PunctualShadows` is that
    # half, and the entry below is the pass whose compose slot it fills.
    #
    # The other two halves are not in this file. `Arena.Paint` names PunctualShadowAtlas behind
    # `ForwardPlus.punctualShadow`, and the `sceneTextures:` line on Main hands over the texture —
    # because a graph resource's barrier belongs to the pass that declared it read, so this node can
    # publish its tile buffer, its sampler and its biases and cannot publish its own atlas.
    #
    # The stage is the sun's: a caster is a caster, and an object drawn depth-only for a cascade is
    # the same object drawn depth-only for a lamp. What differs is the view, and a view is what this
    # node makes sixteen of.
    - !PunctualShadows
      name: Lamps
      stage: Shadow
      atlas: PunctualShadowAtlas
      resolution: 256
      tilesPerSide: 11
      passes:
        - ForwardPlus.PunctualShadowAtlas

    # ── doc 22: the virtualized traversal and the visibility buffer ─────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ **Off, and off because the path was all cost and no pixels.** Three facts, each sufficient:
    #
    #   1. Nothing mounts it. Arena.SupplyFrame builds a VirtualGeometrySystem but never sets
    #      WorldRenderer.Clusters, so extraction's clustered branch never fires and the resolve
    #      draws nothing — the traversal and raster ran every frame for an empty list.
    #   2. Its colour was discarded by construction: the resolve wrote SceneHdr, and !Sky below
    #      overwrites every pixel of SceneHdr before Main loads it.
    #   3. The "shared depth" this document claimed was false: Main declares no depthLoad, the
    #      default is Clear, so the visibility depth died at Main's first pixel anyway.
    #
    # Re-enabling is a checklist, not a flag: mount WorldRenderer.Clusters; move !Sky above
    # !VisibilityBuffer and make the resolve load rather than clear SceneHdr; add SceneAlbedo,
    # SceneNormals and SceneSpecular to Main's `loaded:` and `depthLoad: Load` beside them; and compose the same
    # shadow shaders behind VisibilityResolve's slots that Arena.Paint gives ForwardPlus — the
    # resolve keeps NoDirectionalShadows/NoPunctualShadows today, which next to a shadowed forward
    # pixel is a lighting seam along every material boundary.
    - !ClusterCulling
      name: Traversal
      enabled: false

    # ⚠ `albedo:`/`normals:` are the split on the resolve's side, and they are named or the two
    # paths disagree per pixel: naming both puts the resolve on ForwardPlus.SplitOutputs' exact
    # terms — direct light into the colour, albedo and normals to planes of their own, diffuse
    # ambient left to the !AmbientCombine below. One knob without the other is a loud refusal
    # rather than a half-split frame, which the node itself enforces.
    - !VisibilityBuffer
      name: Visibility
      enabled: false
      output: VisibilityBuffer
      depth: SceneDepth
      colour: SceneHdr
      albedo: SceneAlbedo
      normals: SceneNormals
      specular: SceneSpecular

    # ── the background, which is the environment cube and not a colour ──────────────────────────
    # ⚠ Before the pass that draws the scene, filling a target that pass then *loads*. That is what
    # puts the sky behind the level, and it is why `output` names SceneHdr rather than declaring one
    # of its own: a node with its own target would need a composite to get it into the frame.
    #
    # This is the node kind a document needed before a frame could have a sky at all. The environment
    # cube is baked before the graph exists and outlives every frame, so no `sceneTextures:` line can
    # reach it — what can is set 0, which `Sky.rvn` declares the cube in and a full-screen pass
    # already binds. The background and the ambient are now the same texels, so moving the sun
    # changes both or neither.
    - !Sky
      name: Sky
      output: SceneHdr
      view: Camera

    # ── the classic pass, and today the only one that draws the level: the visibility pair above
    #    is disabled, so this pass clears and owns SceneDepth and the split planes outright. When
    #    the pair returns, depth becomes shared — `depthLoad: Load` here, on the re-enable
    #    checklist above — because a resolve with a depth of its own would be two frames
    #    composited.
    # ⚠ Member order is target order, and the shader dictates it: with SplitOutputs on (Arena.Paint
    # again) ForwardPlus writes location 0 = direct light and emissive, 1 = albedo with the material's
    # occlusion in alpha, 2 = world normal with roughness in alpha, 3 = the surface's f0. Diffuse
    # ambient is in none of them — the combine below owns it, which is the whole split. Reordering
    # this list is not a different layout, it is albedo shaded as radiance through the entire chain,
    # which is why SceneSpecular was appended and not slotted in beside its neighbours.
    #
    # ⚠ Specular ambient is in none of them either, now that the f0 plane exists: the combine adds
    # the traced reflection weighted by that f0 instead, and the pass holds its own prefiltered-cube
    # term back so the dusk sky is not counted twice. The two halves are one switch —
    # AmbientCombineRenderer.Build — and naming `specular:` below is what throws it.
    - !RenderPass
      name: Main
      colourTargets: [SceneHdr, SceneAlbedo, SceneNormals, SceneSpecular]
      depthTarget: SceneDepth

      # ⚠ Per target, and it has to be. SceneHdr keeps what the sky pass above put there — that is
      # the background, and clearing it would throw a full-screen pass away and look exactly like the
      # node not existing. SceneAlbedo, SceneNormals and SceneSpecular are left to `load`, which
      # clears — the
      # visibility resolve above does write both, but its planes go the way its colour already goes
      # (the ordering note at the foot of this file): loading them here on a host with no virtualized
      # geometry wired would be a read of a target no pass produced, which the graph refuses by name,
      # and a frame that loads the planes while the sky overwrites the colour would be half one
      # path's split and half the other's.
      #
      # Block style rather than `[SceneHdr]`, because this reader will not take a comment on the line
      # after a flow sequence — the same quirk `passes:` on the probe field above works around.
      loaded:
        - SceneHdr

      # ⚠ The consuming pass publishes, not the producing one. A graph resource's barrier belongs to
      # whoever declared it read, so the shadow node can hand over its matrices and its sampler and
      # cannot hand over the texture — this line is the other half, and without it set 0's
      # `shadowMap` is still the one-texel stand-in while four cascades are drawn into an atlas
      # nothing samples.
      sceneTextures:
        - binding: shadowMap
          resource: ShadowAtlas

        # And the lamps' atlas, under the compose slot's name rather than the pass's own: a slot's
        # bindings are named for what fills it, so this is `PunctualShadowAtlas.atlas` and the key
        # that reaches set 0 is `ForwardPlus.PunctualShadowAtlas.atlas`. Spell it any other way and
        # the binding a variant declared is one nothing writes, which is a set written short — every
        # draw in this pass refused, rather than a shadow that does not appear.
        - binding: PunctualShadowAtlas.atlas
          resource: PunctualShadowAtlas

      children:
        - !SingleStage
          name: Opaque
          view: Camera
          stage: Opaque

    # ── the ground the arena stands on ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ **Here and not in a `!StandardFrame`'s `extensions: afterOpaque`, because this document has
    # no extension points — every node in it is written out.** That splice's seat is "after the Main
    # pass, sharing its depth, before velocity and particles", and this is that seat spelled by hand:
    # opaque ground belongs with the opaque geometry, and after rather than before because the
    # arena's own floor and walls are the near occluders that should already be in the depth buffer
    # when 252 m of heightfield is rasterised behind them.
    #
    # Every name here is a default of `TerrainNodeAsset` and written out anyway: this frame's
    # SceneHdr, SceneDepth, SceneAlbedo, SceneNormals and ShadowAtlas happen to be spelled the way
    # the node expects, and a document that renamed one would fail at build with a resource nothing
    # produced. Saying them makes the dependency visible rather than lucky.
    #
    # `depthLoad` is not a knob the node has and does not need one: it loads the depth it is given
    # and writes it, which is what an opaque pass after another opaque pass does.
    #
    # ⚠ The host half is `TerrainFactory` in ThirdPersonShooterGame.OnConfigure. Registering the
    # factory is the whole installation — the world renderer's terrain list, the extraction bridge
    # and the asset source that turns a TerrainComponent's reference into a heightfield all come with
    # it. Without that line this node's type is a name nothing in the build claims, which is a
    # refusal from inside the compositor's own construction and not a frame that quietly lacks
    # ground.
    #
    # `grass: true` is the scatter, off the terrain's own Grass weight layer through the
    # TerrainGrassComponent beside the terrain in the scene. Its blades carry no albedo — nothing in
    # Vixen.Rendering.Terrain assigns GrassDrawPass.Albedo — so what this exercises is the scatter,
    # the wind and the cull rather than a textured card. See Assets/Terrain/Outskirts.vxgrass.
    - !Terrain
      name: Ground
      output: SceneHdr
      depth: SceneDepth
      albedo: SceneAlbedo
      normals: SceneNormals
      specular: SceneSpecular
      shadowAtlas: ShadowAtlas
      view: Camera
      grass: true

    # ── the lake's geometry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Doc 35 § D4's quadtree, drawn into two planes and no colour: a coverage mask with the surface's
    # own device depth, and the surface's world normal with its foam. The composite two hundred lines
    # down is what turns those into water.
    #
    # ⚠ **It goes here, immediately after the ground, and its own remarks say why:** the surface is
    # rasterised against the scene's own depth so a lake behind a hill is hidden by it, and that depth
    # has just been written by !Main and !Ground. Its depth state is Greater with no write and it
    # *loads* rather than clears, so against a depth buffer that is not yet real every fragment fails
    # the test and the pass draws nothing — with no validation error anywhere. That is this module's
    # own history: a fully green device-free suite over a pass that had never rendered a pixel.
    #
    # ⚠ **`Arena.ReportFrame` prints the patch count and the zone count for this node**, because
    # "45 patches drawn" and "the surface plane is empty" are both true at once when the depth test is
    # the thing failing — the counter is patches *submitted*, not fragments that survived.
    - !WaterSurface
      name: Lake
      surface: WaterSurface
      normal: WaterNormal
      sceneDepth: SceneDepth
      view: Camera
      # 64 m of level 0 over five levels reaches 1 km, which is well past this 252 m of ground: the
      # far skirt is what covers the rest and it is drawn without the wave sum.
      nearRange: 64
      levelCount: 5
      farDistance: 2000
      # TerrainSeed.LakeSurface — where the *far* skirt sits, for a camera looking past the window.
      #
      # ⚠ The claim that used to be here — "nothing in this level is beyond the window, so it only
      # ever shows on the horizon" — was checked when the lake first drew and is half true, and the
      # half that is not is a cost rather than a picture. `WaterSurfaceMesh.SelectFar` tiles every
      # frustum-visible cell out to `farDistance` with no coverage test, so this level submits 34
      # skirt patches to the window's 10; every one of them is then occluded, by the arena's walls
      # from inside and by the outskirts from the lake shore, and turning the skirt off (farDistance
      # 0) was measured to give a pixel-identical frame from both viewpoints. Left on because it is
      # what a camera taken above the ridge would want, and recorded here because 34 of 44 patches
      # is what the counter in `Arena.ReportFrame` is mostly counting.
      restHeight: 1.2
      # A quarter-metre of shallows before the water is fully water, forty centimetres before it
      # foams, and half a metre of crest above the rest height before a wave breaks. This lake's
      # shelf rises through all three over about four metres of shore, which is what makes the
      # margin read as a beach rather than as a cut.
      shoreDepth: 0.25
      foamDepth: 0.4
      foamCrest: 0.4


    # ── where everything was last frame ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # The debt Taa.rvn has carried since it was written: it declares a motionVectors input and nothing
    # produced one. Motion blur wants the same texture.
    #
    # ⚠ After Main and sharing its depth, read-only, so the velocity written for a pixel is the
    # velocity of whatever ended up visible there. A pass with its own depth would be a second
    # rasterisation of the same scene disagreeing with the first at every silhouette.
    - !RenderPass
      name: Velocity
      colourTargets: [SceneMotion]
      depthTarget: SceneDepth
      depthLoad: Load
      readOnlyDepth: true

      children:
        - !SingleStage
          name: MotionVectors
          view: Camera
          stage: Motion

    # ── the lamps' embers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A pass of its own rather than a second child of Main, and the reason is Main's *other*
    # attachments. That pass writes SceneHdr, SceneAlbedo, SceneNormals and SceneSpecular, so a
    # pipeline built for it declares four colour targets — and `ParticleSprite` returns one value,
    # which leaves attachments 1 to 3 undefined and additively blended into the albedo, normals and
    # f0 planes. One target here means the question does not arise.
    #
    # Depth is Main's, loaded and read-only, on the velocity pass's terms: an ember behind a wall is
    # rejected by the depth the opaque pass already wrote, and nothing here writes depth back.
    #
    # ⚠ **Before !Accumulate, so the embers go through the whole photometric chain** — the fog, the
    # defocus, the shutter, the meter, the flare, the bloom and the tonemap. That is what makes them
    # read as light rather than as orange dots pasted on: `ParticleSprite.emissive` is in cd/m² like
    # everything else in this level, and a spark at forty thousand nits blooms because `Glow`'s
    # threshold of three thousand is a real number about a real quantity.
    #
    # ⚠ The cost is temporal antialiasing. These carry no motion vectors — the velocity pass above is
    # for meshes, and a particle has no previous world matrix to difference — so TAA reprojects them
    # as though they were still and the neighbourhood clamp is what stops the ghost. At the speeds
    # these drift (a fifth of a metre a second) that reads as a very short trail, which is a thing an
    # ember does. It would not survive a fast spark, and the answer for one is the GPU path, where a
    # particle has a previous position to write.
    - !RenderPass
      name: Sparks
      colourTargets: [SceneHdr]
      depthTarget: SceneDepth
      depthLoad: Load
      readOnlyDepth: true

      # SceneHdr holds the sky and the level by now, and every one of these is added to it.
      loaded:
        - SceneHdr

      children:
        - !SingleStage
          name: Embers
          view: Camera
          stage: Embers

    # ── doc 22: the depth pyramid next frame's cull tests against ───────────────────────────────
    # After every pass that touches SceneDepth — Main writes it, Velocity and Sparks test it — so
    # what the reduce sees is the frame's finished depth. What consumes it is *next* frame's
    # !GpuCulling at the head of this file, which runs before anything is drawn and so can never
    # have anything newer; that one frame of staleness is the whole cost of the one-phase cull.
    #
    # The pyramid itself is the host's (builder.Occluders) — the same object the culling group
    # tests against, because those are one fact seen from either end of a frame boundary and
    # nothing else would keep them the same one. Farthest-reduced, which under reversed depth is
    # the minimum: an object is hidden only when its nearest point is behind everything in the
    # tile. The screen marches skip by a *nearest* chain — a different pyramid on the builder's
    # TracePyramid slot — and confusing the two skips rays through walls.
    - !HiZ
      name: Occluders
      depth: SceneDepth

    # ── doc 22 phase 7: the sun's shadow at the resolution each pixel needs ─────────────────────
    # The second consumer of the frame's finished depth, beside !HiZ above and on its exact terms:
    # what the marking pass reads is this frame's depth and what it produces — a bitset of the pages
    # some pixel asked for — is serviced at the head of the *next* frame, because the dispatch that
    # writes it has been submitted and not waited for. Passes run in declaration order, so this sits
    # after every pass that touches SceneDepth; the page draws and the table upload happen inside
    # the node before the marking, and the shading pass reads a table that only ever names pages
    # drawn in earlier frames — a page is absent until drawn, which is the direction a shadow is
    # allowed to be wrong in.
    #
    # The atlas is the host's (ArenaIllumination fills builder.VirtualShadows), not a resource line
    # above: a shadow page's contents are about the world rather than about a frame, and a graph
    # transient is discarded at the end of the pass that wrote it — a cache that never holds
    # anything. That is also why no `sceneTextures:` line hands the pages to Main: the node owns its
    # texture and publishes it straight into set 0 under the qualified prefix below.
    #
    # The stage is the sun's and the lamps': a caster is a caster, drawn depth-only per page view.
    # ⚠ Which means a *virtualized* mesh casts through the fallback mesh phase 1 generates — the
    # traversal cannot yet cut clusters per page (doc 22 names it as owed) — so the shadow's
    # RESOLUTION is phase 7's and the caster's level of detail is still phase 1's.
    #
    # `passes:` is qualified — the pass, then the shader filling its slot — because a composed
    # slot's bindings are named for what fills it, exactly as !PunctualShadows' entry is. ForwardPlus
    # alone, deliberately: this frame's visibility resolve keeps NoDirectionalShadows (as it keeps
    # NoPunctualShadows — its colour is overwritten by !Sky before Main loads it, see the note at
    # the foot of this file), and publishing under a prefix the resolve never declares would be
    # eight values written for nobody. The other half is Arena.Paint composing VirtualShadowLookup
    # behind ForwardPlus.directionalShadow — the slot without the passes line is a set written
    # short, the passes line without the slot is a map rendered for nobody.
    - !VirtualShadow
      name: SunPages
      stage: Shadow
      depth: SceneDepth
      view: Camera
      levels: 8
      firstExtent: 10.0
      depthRange: 400.0
      pagesPerFrame: 16
      passes:
        - ForwardPlus.VirtualShadowLookup

    # ── doc 19 § L3: the screen-probe gather ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # On. The two gaps that kept it off are both closed, and both closed at the ambient split's
    # door exactly as the old comment here promised: placement's decode takes Depth32Float and
    # Rgba16Float directly now, and SceneNormals has a producer — the Main pass's third attachment.
    # The host slots were filled an increment before the flip — ArenaIllumination wires the tracer,
    # resolver, accumulator and filter, with the clipmap behind the field slot, the probe field
    # behind the far slot, the surface cache behind the hit slot and the Preetham average as the
    # miss — so this node was one `enabled:` line the whole time, which was the point of wiring
    # early.
    #
    # `output` names a target of its own rather than SceneHdr, deliberately: what the upsample
    # draws is irradiance over π, which wants multiplying by albedo — and the !AmbientCombine below
    # is the pass that multiplies. Adding it straight onto a lit frame would be ambient with no
    # albedo in it, painted over ambient the material already applied.
    #
    # `screenTraces: true` is what makes this node the second taker of the builder's nearest chain
    # — the !Reflections below is the other — and TakeTracePyramid sizes the ring for both, which
    # the frame tests pin at exactly two.
    #
    # ⚠ `screenLinearThickness` is the shell in METRES, and leaving it unset is not a neutral
    # default — it is the whole hit test collapsing. Unset, the march keeps the device-depth shell
    # of 0.02, and under reverse-Z with this camera's 0.1/1000 planes device depth is
    # `z = -1.0001e-4 + 0.10001/d`: the shell reaches from a surface at `d` back to
    # `0.10001/(z - 0.02 + 1.0001e-4)`, which is *negative* — past the far plane — for every
    # `z < 0.02`, that is for every surface further away than 4.98 m. The frame's median surface
    # sits at 5–8 m and its p10 at 32 m, so across most of the picture the test "is this sample
    # inside that surface" degenerates into "is this sample behind that surface at all", and a ray
    # sailing thirty metres past a wall reports a hit on it. Indirect light with the right colour,
    # arriving from the wrong place, which is the class of wrong answer that looks plausible.
    #
    # One metre is the arena's own number rather than a knob turned until the frame looked nice:
    # Arena.vxscene authors the floor slab, all four walls and all four pillars at `halfExtents`
    # 0.5 on their thin axis, so one metre is the thickness of every solid that bounds this room.
    # The crates are thicker (1.6 m) and the cover panels thinner (0.3 m), and a shell sized to
    # the room's own walls is the one that survives someone adding another wall.
    - !ScreenProbeGather
      name: Gather
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      output: ProbeIrradiance
      view: Camera
      tileSize: 16
      screenTraces: true
      screenLinearThickness: 1.0
      planeTolerance: 0.02

    # ── doc 19 § L5: traced reflections ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # On — the last of this page's nodes to flip, and the flip was exactly the three lines its old
    # comment promised: this `enabled:`, the combine's `reflections:` seat, and the lock test,
    # together. The seam that kept it off closed in the engine: ReflectionRenderer `frame.Add`s
    # its target into the frame's namespace now (the same call the gather and the occlusion pair
    # always made), so `Reflections` is a name the combine below resolves rather than a
    # CompositorBindingException out of every frame's build.
    #
    # The two old gaps stay closed and are worth restating so nobody re-litigates them: the
    # kernel's normals contract (world normal in xyz, roughness in alpha) is what the Main pass's
    # third attachment writes, and the composite this answer lands in is the !AmbientCombine's
    # `reflections:` seat, blended by validity alpha.
    #
    # The host half was wired an increment before the flip — ArenaIllumination.Feed configures the
    # Trace's composed sources the frame the node first builds (clipmap march, surface-cache hits,
    # irradiance-field rough answers, sky misses) — so turning this on was one line here and none
    # there, the same story the gather already told.
    #
    # ⚠ `colour: SceneHdr` is deliberate scheduling: at this point in the frame SceneHdr holds the
    # sky, the level and the sparks, so a screen hit reflects this frame's own lit opaques. This
    # node is also the second taker of the builder's nearest chain — the gather above is the other
    # — and TakeTracePyramid sizes the ring for both, which the frame tests pin at exactly two.
    #
    # ⚠ `screenLinearThickness` for the same reason the gather above carries it, and this is the
    # node where the defect is *visible* rather than diffuse: a diffuse gather averages a wrong-place
    # hit into thirty-two others, a mirror shows it. The same metre, from the same walls.
    - !Reflections
      name: Mirrors
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      colour: SceneHdr
      target: Reflections
      view: Camera
      roughnessThreshold: 0.45
      screenSteps: 32
      screenLinearThickness: 1.0

    # ── doc 19's two screen passes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # `source` is the *type* bound to this pass's `distanceField` compose slot — see Clipmap above
    # for the other half of the pair.
    #
    # On, because the two gaps that kept this group disabled are the two the ambient split closed:
    # SceneNormals has a producer — the Main pass's third attachment — so the march stands on real
    # normals, and something finally consumes an occlusion buffer, the !AmbientCombine below, which
    # multiplies this plane's `.r` into the ambient it rebuilds. For a long time these nodes were
    # kept here disabled because a node that runs and is ignored costs a march per pixel for a
    # texture the graph throws away; what closed that was not a line in this file, exactly as the
    # old comment predicted — it was the shading pass learning to write its ambient to a target of
    # its own.
    #
    # ⚠ `sunShadow: false`, because the sun is already shadowed. The `.g` channel this march can
    # fill is sun visibility, and the combine multiplies it into the *direct* term — but every
    # direct texel in this frame already went through the !ShadowMap cascades, so a cone toward the
    # sun here would shadow the sun twice, softer and wronger. Occlusion in `.r` is the whole job;
    # the channel rule (occlusion on ambient, visibility on direct) lives in DistanceFieldAoRenderer
    # and the combine keeps it.
    #
    # ⚠ One AO march, not two. `Arena.Paint` used to compile this same clipmap march into the
    # material (`UseDistanceFieldOcclusion`), where in split mode it lands in albedo's alpha — and
    # the combine multiplies albedo.a *and* this plane into the same ambient, which would be the
    # room's occlusion squared. That flag is off now, and the why is written beside it; this node
    # is the survivor because a half-resolution screen march with a bilateral upsample costs less
    # than a march per shaded pixel and answers the same question.
    # `view: Camera` on the gather's terms: the march reconstructs world positions from depth, and
    # with nothing driving the unprojection every pixel stands at a place that exists nowhere. The
    # knob is new — ArenaIllumination.Feed used to push the inverse (and set 0) by hand every
    # frame, and the engine owns both halves now.
    - !DistanceFieldAo
      name: Occlusion
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      source: GlobalDistanceField
      output: AmbientOcclusion
      sunShadow: false
      view: Camera

    # ⚠ Off, and off because it is *redundant*, which is a new reason on this page: the gather
    # above already supplies the combine's screen irradiance, with real traces behind it where this
    # pass has only the probe volumes — running both is the same skylight added twice.
    - !IndirectDiffuse
      name: Indirect
      enabled: false
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      output: IndirectDiffuse
      intensity: 1.0

    # The screen-space companion, on with its partner. GTAO is the contact darkening in a crease;
    # the march above is the room. The combine multiplies the two — `dfao.r × ssao.r` — into the
    # ambient it rebuilds, which is exactly the "both, multiplied" this comment used to promise.
    - !Ssao
      name: ContactOcclusion
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      view: Camera
      output: ScreenOcclusion
      directions: 8
      steps: 6
      radius: 0.6
      intensity: 1.1
      falloff: 1.0
      scale: 0.5

    # ── the split, put back together ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # The consumer the whole page above has been building toward, and the one place diffuse ambient
    # happens now: `direct × sun + albedo × irradiance × occlusion`, a sky pixel — zero normal —
    # passed through untouched. Every plane it names is produced above it: the split targets by the
    # Main pass (and the visibility resolve, on the terms the Main pass comment states), irradiance
    # by the gather, the two occlusions by the pair just before it.
    #
    # ⚠ Before !TemporalAntialiasing, necessarily. The TAA averages radiance, and half-lit radiance
    # — direct with the ambient still owed — is not a darker version of the finished frame, it is a
    # different image whose history would be blended into the finished one at every disocclusion.
    # The combine is where the frame becomes whole, so it is the earliest point the accumulator is
    # allowed to see.
    #
    # `reflections:` is the !Reflections node's target, blended over the rebuilt frame by validity
    # alpha — the seat this document held empty until the renderer could publish the plane into
    # the frame's namespace. A sharp ray that hit answers with radiance and validity one; a texel
    # the trace could not answer stays the frame's own, which is what the alpha blend is for.
    #
    # `occlusion:` carries dfao's plane, whose `.g` is one everywhere because that node's
    # `sunShadow` is off — see there for why the cascades own the sun. `intensity` multiplies the
    # rebuilt ambient alone, and one is the honest number: the meter and the tonemap already own
    # the frame's brightness, and a knob here quietly re-grading the shade would be a second
    # exposure wearing a lighting term's clothes.
    #
    # `depth:` and `view:` are the bilateral upsample's — the promise the !DistanceFieldAo comment
    # above has been making. Both AO planes arrive at half resolution, and this node is the one
    # place they meet the full-resolution frame: with a depth and a camera the combine reads each
    # plane as its four nearest texels weighted by the probe upsample's plane test, instead of a
    # linear tap that smears occlusion across the depth edge at every corner.
    - !AmbientCombine
      name: Combine
      direct: SceneHdr
      albedo: SceneAlbedo
      normals: SceneNormals

      # ⚠ Half of one change with `reflections:` above it, and neither half is safe alone. With both,
      # the shading pass stops writing its prefiltered-cube specular and this adds the traced plane
      # weighted by the surface's own f0 — about 0.55 on the ramps rather than a dielectric's 0.04.
      # With only one of them the frame either counts the sky twice or reflects a metal as paint, so
      # the node requires both before it moves anything.
      specular: SceneSpecular
      irradiance: ProbeIrradiance
      occlusion: AmbientOcclusion
      contactOcclusion: ScreenOcclusion
      reflections: Reflections
      depth: SceneDepth
      view: Camera
      output: SceneCombined
      intensity: 1.0

    # ── the lake ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Doc 35's pass, spliced where § D8 puts it: after the lighting is finished and before anything
    # temporal or tonal. Three nodes, and the order between them is not a preference.
    #
    # ⚠ **`!WaterSurface` sits up beside `!Terrain`, not down here, and that is its own remarks'
    # instruction rather than a preference:** the surface is rasterised against the scene's own depth,
    # so it belongs where that depth has just been written and long before the copy. What is down here
    # is only the pair that needs the *lit* frame — the copy and the composite.
    #
    # ⚠ **Against an *undefined* depth buffer the surface pass draws nothing at all with no
    # validation error anywhere.** Its depth state is
    # Greater with no write and it loads rather than clears — the surface joins a frame that already
    # has its opaque depth — so every fragment fails the test if the buffer has not been written. This
    # module's history is a fully green device-free test suite over a pass that had never rendered a
    # pixel, and the way it drew one was a fixture that cleared the depth first. Here the depth is
    # real: !Main and !Ground both wrote it, hundreds of lines up, and a lake behind a hill is
    # correctly hidden by that same test.
    #
    # ⚠ **The copy is the whole of § B1, and it is not fussiness.** Reading the target a pass is also
    # writing is undefined behaviour on a driver rather than approximate, which is why the blocker was
    # named as the copy and not as the pass. `behind:` must name the copy and never SceneCombined; the
    # graph refuses the case where the two are equal.
    #
    # ⚠ **And the copy is what makes `tiled: true` correct.** Tile classification leaves a dry tile's
    # pixels exactly as it found them, so the tiled picture equals the untiled one only where the
    # output already holds what `behind:` is a copy of — which is true here *because* the !Copy filled
    # it from this very target one node ago. A hand-wired node without one is a frame with water in
    # the wet tiles and last frame's contents in the dry ones.
    - !Copy
      name: WaterBehind
      source: SceneCombined
      destination: SceneCombinedCopy

    - !Water
      name: Water
      output: SceneCombined
      behind: SceneCombinedCopy
      sceneDepth: SceneDepth
      surface: WaterSurface
      normal: WaterNormal
      view: Camera
      tiled: true
      # ⚠ **These three are overwritten every frame by `Arena.FeedWater`, and they have to be.**
      # `sunDirection`, `sunColour` and `skyColour` are the *radiance* the volume scatters, in the
      # frame's own units — this level's sun is about twenty thousand and its sky is thousands — and
      # what a document can write by hand is a tint around one. A tint here is not a slightly wrong
      # lake: the integration is exactly right and lands four decades under the exposure, so the water
      # tonemaps to the same black as the shaded ground it sits in, with the fold, the patch counts,
      # the info texture and the composite's build count all reporting success. That is task #119, and
      # it cost a week because a lake that is four decades too dim and a water pass that never ran are
      # pixel-for-pixel the same picture.
      #
      # ⚠ And the level's sun *moves* — a SunOrbit turns it once every thirty seconds — so a direction
      # stated here is right at one moment of the orbit and wrong for the rest of it. Both halves are
      # `WaterRenderer.LightFrom`, called per frame.
      #
      # What is left below is the fallback for an editor or a host that does not push: eight degrees
      # up, travelling north-east, which is this level's premise and the reason everything inside the
      # walls is in shade.
      sunDirection: -0.5698 -0.1400 0.8098
      sunColour: 1.0 0.72 0.42
      skyColour: 0.36 0.30 0.30
      # An inland lake: more scattering and less absorption than open sea, which is what makes
      # shallow water read as green rather than as a darker version of the sky.
      scattering: 0.05 0.09 0.10
      absorption: 0.42 0.09 0.04
      phaseG: 0.7
      surfaceF0: 0.02
      foam: true

    # ── the accumulator ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Where the motion vector pass finally pays for itself twice. TAA reprojects last frame's colour
    # through `SceneMotion` and blends it in, which is what turns one sample per pixel per frame into
    # eight over eight frames.
    #
    # ⚠ In HDR and before every curve, which is why it sits here and not after the tonemap. A
    # temporal resolve averages radiance; averaging *tonemapped* values averages the outputs of a
    # curve, and a bright edge moving across a dark wall converges to the wrong grey.
    #
    # `varianceClipping` is what stops the reprojection smearing: a history sample outside the
    # neighbourhood's colour distribution is a sample of something that is no longer there.
    #
    # `source` is the combine's output rather than SceneHdr, and the difference between the two is
    # the difference between a frame and a frame missing its diffuse ambient — see the node above
    # for why the accumulator must only ever see the finished one.
    - !TemporalAntialiasing
      name: Accumulate
      source: SceneCombined
      motionVectors: SceneMotion
      depth: SceneDepth
      output: SceneResolved
      varianceClipping: true
      feedback: 0.9
      varianceGamma: 1.25

    # ── the air between the camera and the level ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Depth fog, and it is the cheapest thing in this document that makes a distance read as one.
    #
    # ⚠ Before the shutter and before every curve, because fog is light *in the scene*: it is added
    # to what a surface sent, so a blurred lantern and the haze in front of it smear together.
    #
    # The colour is the horizon's own — the sky above is Preetham at 2.6 turbidity with the sun six
    # degrees up, so the air near the ground is that orange rather than the blue-grey a default fog
    # assumes. `sunScattering` is what makes looking west brighter than looking east.
    - !Fog
      name: Air
      source: SceneResolved
      depth: SceneDepth
      view: Camera
      output: SceneFogged
      mode: 2
      heightFalloff: true
      sunScattering: true
      colour: 0.42 0.30 0.20
      density: 0.006

    # ── what the lens cannot hold in focus ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # `view: Camera` is the whole configuration: the circle of confusion comes from the camera's own
    # `aperture`, `focalLength` and `focusDistance`, so a defocus that disagreed with the exposure
    # would be two opinions about one diaphragm.
    #
    # ⚠ `focusDistance` on the camera is zero in this sample, which `Camera` documents as "focus at
    # infinity" — so this pass is very nearly a copy until something drives it. It is here so that a
    # game that racks focus has the node to rack, and `useBladeShape` gives the bokeh the same six
    # blades the starburst below uses.
    - !DepthOfField
      name: Defocus
      source: SceneFogged
      depth: SceneDepth
      view: Camera
      output: SceneDefocused
      samples: 16
      useBladeShape: true
      maximumRadius: 16.0

    # ── the shutter ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ Before the tonemap and before the bloom, because a smear is light landing on the sensor over
    # an interval — averaging it after the curve averages two curves' outputs, and a lantern crossing
    # a dark wall would smear grey rather than bright.
    #
    # There is no intensity. `view: Camera` is what supplies the shutter: the camera's `shutterTime`
    # already decides the exposure, and this is the second thing it decides — so a frame cannot be
    # bright in a way its blur disagrees with.
    - !MotionBlur
      name: Shutter
      source: SceneDefocused
      motionVectors: SceneMotion
      view: Camera
      output: SceneBlurred
      samples: 8
      maximumRadius: 24.0

    # ── the meter ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A histogram of the frame's log luminance, reduced on the GPU and adapted over time. Nothing
    # about it is a picture: it publishes one number in a buffer, which the tonemap below names.
    #
    # ⚠ **The bounds are what keep this a meter and not a director.** Almost every surface in this
    # level is in the shadow of a six-metre wall under a sun six degrees up, so a meter asked for an
    # unbounded answer renders the shade as middle grey — which is what a meter is *for* and is not
    # what anybody means by dusk, and is the whole argument the tonemap's own `ev100` makes below.
    # These clamp the adaptation to about a stop either side of the authored EV 13, so walking under
    # a floodlight and back out is an adaptation rather than a re-grade.
    #
    # `lowPercentile`/`highPercentile` are why the histogram is worth its dispatches over the mip
    # reduction: the darkest half a per cent of a frame is a lantern's shadow and the brightest five
    # is the sun on a wall, and an average over both is an exposure that neither wants.
    #
    # ⚠ This was the node that found `ComputeRenderer.Composition`. It is the only compute shader in
    # the post-effect library and so the only one a frame reaches through that path, and every
    # `!Compute` node in every document was refused until it named the default composition — see
    # there. What it looked like was an unhandled exception from inside the frame's build, listing
    # compose slots in files this document has never mentioned.
    - !AutoExposure
      name: Meter
      source: SceneBlurred
      useHistogram: true
      middleGrey: 0.18
      brightenRate: 1.2
      darkenRate: 0.6
      minimumLogLuminance: -4.0
      maximumLogLuminance: 18.0
      lowPercentile: 0.5
      highPercentile: 0.95
      minimumExposure: 0.0000509
      maximumExposure: 0.0002035

    # ── one exposure per region, rather than one for the whole frame ───────────────────────────
    # The case this level *is*: a sun six degrees up throws a fifty-seven metre shadow, so almost
    # every surface is in shade and the few that are not are two hundred times brighter. One exposure
    # cannot hold both — which is why `ev100` below is a stop and a half under what a meter asks for,
    # and why the shade still reads as flat. This brings the sunlit faces down and the shade up,
    # around a pivot that is the same exposure the tonemap will apply.
    #
    # ⚠ `ev100` here has to agree with the tonemap's. The pivot is the luminance that stays exactly
    # where it is; set it somewhere else and this is a global exposure change wearing a local one's
    # clothes.
    #
    # ⚠ `edgeRange` is what decides whether there are halos. Wide, and a floodlight's pool bleeds
    # across the wall beside it so the wall is compressed as though it were lit.
    - !LocalExposure
      name: Adapt
      source: SceneBlurred
      output: SceneAdapted
      #
      # ⚠ **`edgeRange` is the halo, and 1.5 was wide enough to see.** This is the range term of a
      # bilateral blur: it decides how different in luminance two pixels may be and still be averaged
      # together. Wide, and the blur crosses the edge of a floodlight's pool, so the wall beside the
      # pool is compressed as though it were lit — and a region compressed by an amount that falls
      # off with distance from the pool *is* a ring. Narrow, and the blur stops at the edge, which is
      # what an edge-aware filter is for. The two contrasts are how hard whatever survives is pushed,
      # so they come down with it.
      #
      # ⚠ Twelve taps rather than six. A bilateral blur with too few taps over a high-contrast region
      # does not read as "blurry", it reads as *structure* — the taps land on a coarse pattern and the
      # exposure follows it, which over a bright pool on a dark floor is a grid of squares. This is
      # one of the three suspects for the artefact the level still has; see the note at the foot of
      # this file for how to tell them apart.
      ev100: 13.0
      taps: 12
      blurRadius: 6.0
      edgeRange: 0.55
      highlightContrast: 0.22
      shadowContrast: 0.16

    # ── what the lens does to the rest of the frame ────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Ghosts of the sun and of the floodlight globes, on the line through the centre of the frame.
    # Not bloom: bloom is light scattered a short way from where it landed, and this is light that
    # bounced between two elements and came out somewhere else entirely.
    #
    # ⚠ `threshold` is in cd/m², like the bloom's below and for the same reason. Forty thousand is
    # about a quarter of a floodlight lens and well under the sky beside the sun, so what ghosts is
    # the handful of things that would ghost through a real lens.
    #
    # `view: Camera` gives the starburst the camera's own blade count — the same six blades that
    # shape the bokeh in a defocus pass. One diaphragm, two effects.
    - !LensFlare
      name: Flare
      source: SceneAdapted
      view: Camera
      output: SceneFlared
      #
      # ⚠ `useHalo` off, and it defaults *on* — which is what put a literal ring around every bright
      # thing in the frame. A flare halo is an annulus by construction: a ring of radius
      # `haloRadius` about the centre of the frame, brightest at `haloThickness` either side of it.
      # Under fifteen floodlights it is fifteen rings. The ghosts and the starburst are the parts of
      # a real lens worth having here; a halo wants a much dimmer scene than this one.
      threshold: 40000.0
      ghosts: 4
      ghostSpacing: 0.32
      ghostIntensity: 0.05
      useHalo: false
      haloIntensity: 0.0
      chromaticOffset: 0.007
      useStarburst: true
      starburstIntensity: 0.4

    # ── what the lanterns spill ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ `threshold` is in the *source's* units, and the source is scene radiance in cd/m². This is
    # not the usual bloom threshold of one: nothing in a physically lit frame is near one, and a
    # threshold of one here would bloom the floor. Three thousand is about two stops over what
    # `ev100` below calls middle grey and about twice the brightest sunlit surface, so what spills is
    # the floodlight lenses at forty thousand nits and the sky beside the sun — which is what a lens
    # really does.
    #
    # It publishes the pyramid and nothing else. The tonemap is what composites it; see there.
    - !Bloom
      name: Glow
      source: SceneFlared
      output: BloomPyramid
      levels: 6
      threshold: 3000.0
      knee: 1500.0
      filterRadius: 2.0
      intensity: 1.0

    # ── the frame ends here: radiance in, the swapchain's eight bits out ────────────────────────
    # ⚠ `source` is the HDR target and `output` is the imported swapchain image, and this document
    # used to read `Bloom` in `source`. That was a black screen, and a subtle one: !Bloom publishes
    # the *pyramid* — the blurred part of the image above `threshold` — and not the scene with a glow
    # added. Tonemapping it alone throws the scene away, and a level lit below a threshold of one
    # leaves a pyramid of zeroes. Every counter in the run said the frame drew.
    #
    # `bloom:` is the fix, and it is a different line from `source:` on purpose — the two cannot be
    # confused for one another now. The pyramid is added to the scene in linear light *before* the
    # exposure and the curve, because bloom is light the lens spread across the image: added after
    # the curve it could not blow out, and a highlight already at white would somehow gain a halo
    # brighter than itself.
    #
    # ⚠ `ev100` and not `exposure`, and with a scene lit in lux and lumens it is the only one of the
    # two that means anything. Luminances here run from a few hundredths in the deep shadow to
    # thousands on a lantern globe — multiplying that by any number an author would think to type
    # gives either white or black. An exposure value names the luminance that comes out as middle
    # grey: 15 is bright sun, 12 is overcast, 10 is a lit room after dark. A meter pointed at this
    # level — a sun six degrees up delivering fourteen thousand lux, a sky of about twelve hundred
    # cd/m², fifteen sodium floodlights of a hundred to two hundred and sixty thousand lumens — asks
    # for 11.6, and this is 13.0.
    #
    # ⚠ Nearly a stop and a half under, and that is the whole of "moody" rather than a fudge. Six
    # metres of wall against a sun six degrees up throws a shadow fifty-seven metres long, so almost
    # the entire arena is in shade and the exposure that renders the shade correctly renders it as
    # mid-grey — which is what a meter is for and is not what anybody means by dusk. Underexposing
    # puts the few sunlit faces where they would be anyway and lets everything they do not reach fall
    # away, so a shadow reads as an absence of light rather than as a slightly darker grey, and a
    # floodlight's pool is the brightest thing on the floor beneath it.
    #
    # ⚠ `exposureBuffer` is what makes the meter above mean something, and it is the line that did
    # not exist: `TonemapRenderer.ExposureBuffer` has been there since the meter was written and no
    # `!Tonemap` could name it, so a document could run the histogram, reduce it, adapt it and then
    # tonemap with the number somebody typed — with every counter in the run saying the meter ran.
    # A buffer here wins over `ev100`, which is why the meter's bounds are drawn around it rather
    # than left open.
    #
    # `useColorGrading` turns on the four-range decision list, which is the last thing before the
    # curve: shadows toward the sky's blue, midtones left alone, highlights toward the sodium the
    # floodlights actually are. Off, the whole grade folds out of the compiled variant — so a
    # document that authors ranges and forgets this line gets the picture it had before.
    - !Tonemap
      name: Tonemap
      source: SceneFlared
      bloom: BloomPyramid
      bloomIntensity: 0.5
      exposureBuffer: Meter.Exposure
      output: SceneGraded
      operator: 1
      ev100: 13.0
      whitePoint: 4.0
      contrast: 1.06
      saturation: 1.12
      useColorGrading: true

      # ⚠ **Every member of every range is written out, and leaving one off is not a default.**
      # `ColorGradingRange` is a struct whose zero is a saturation of zero, a contrast of zero and a
      # gain of zero — a black greyscale image — and `ColorGradingRange.Neutral` is a property rather
      # than that zero for exactly this reason. A reader fills what a document names and leaves the
      # rest at the struct's zero, so a range that names only `saturation` is not "that saturation
      # and nothing else": it is that saturation over a black frame.
      grading:
        shadowsMax: 0.09
        highlightsMin: 0.5

        # Nothing globally. The three ranges below partition the image — their weights sum to one at
        # every luminance — so a global term here would be a fourth opinion over all three.
        global: { saturation: 1.0, contrast: 1.0, gamma: 1 1 1, gain: 1 1 1, offset: 0 0 0 }

        # The shade is lit by the sky and the sky is blue, so this is the picture agreeing with its
        # own lighting rather than a look: a touch of blue lifted into the deepest values, and a
        # little colour taken out where there is not enough light to see any.
        shadows: { saturation: 0.92, contrast: 1.0, gamma: 1 1 1, gain: 1.0 1.0 1.03, offset: 0.004 0.006 0.012 }

        # Left alone. Most of the arena is midtone, and a grade that moves everything is an exposure.
        midtones: { saturation: 1.0, contrast: 1.0, gamma: 1 1 1, gain: 1 1 1, offset: 0 0 0 }

        # And toward the sodium the floodlights are: 1850–2200 K is orange, so the brightest parts of
        # the frame lean that way and gain a little saturation for being the only saturated thing.
        highlights: { saturation: 1.06, contrast: 1.0, gamma: 1 1 1, gain: 1.05 1.0 0.93, offset: 0 0 0 }

    # ── the lens's own artefacts, and they are the last thing that happens ─────────────────────
    # Everything below is in the swapchain's eight bits, which is the point: an antialiaser looks
    # for edges the eye sees, and the eye sees them after the curve.
    #
    # ⚠ FXAA *and* the temporal resolve above, which is not the usual pair. TAA converges a still
    # frame and cannot help the first frame after a cut or a fragment its history clipped; this
    # catches what is left, and at a threshold this high it is nearly free on everything else.
    - !Fxaa
      name: Edges
      source: SceneGraded
      output: SceneAntialiased
      subpixel: true
      edgeThreshold: 0.166
      edgeThresholdMinimum: 0.0833
      subpixelQuality: 0.6

    # What the temporal resolve softened, put back. A contrast-adaptive sharpen rather than an
    # unsharp mask, so a flat wall gains nothing and an edge gains what TAA took.
    #
    # ⚠ 0.35 was re-judged once the camera's sub-pixel offset was actually applied, because until
    # then this node was arguably compensating for a resolve that averaged the same sample over and
    # over rather than for genuine supersampling. It is not: measured at 256 frames, taking it to
    # zero moves the wall-top staircase by 0.001 px (0.1667 against 0.1658) and costs 20% of the
    # frame's acutance (1.570 against 1.307). It is adding micro-contrast in texture, not
    # re-aliasing silhouettes, which is what a contrast-adaptive sharpen is for — it backs off
    # exactly where the resolve did its work. Left where it is.
    - !Sharpen
      name: Recover
      source: SceneAntialiased
      output: SceneSharpened
      perChannel: true
      sharpness: 0.35

    # ── the glass ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Four things one node does because they are all the same lens: the falloff toward the corners,
    # the colour fringing where the elements do not focus every wavelength on one plane, the barrel
    # distortion of a wide prime, and the grain of the sensor holding all of it.
    #
    # ⚠ `output: SceneColour`, so this and not the tonemap is what the swapchain gets. The resource
    # is declared *and* imported at the top of this file — the import wins — which is what lets this
    # document build against a scratch texture in a test and write the window at run time.
    #
    # The numbers are deliberately under what a stylised game would use. Grain that reads as grain
    # is a decision about the picture; grain at 0.02, luminance-weighted so it stays out of the
    # highlights, is what stops a smooth gradient banding in eight bits.
    - !Vignette
      name: Glass
      source: SceneSharpened
      output: SceneColour
      useVignette: true
      useChromaticAberration: true
      useGrain: false
      luminanceWeightedGrain: true
      vignetteIntensity: 0.28
      vignetteSmoothness: 0.55
      vignetteRoundness: 0.9
      aberrationStrength: 0.0018
      grainIntensity: 0.02
      grainScale: 1.0
      useLensDistortion: true
      distortionIntensity: -0.04
      distortionZoom: 1.02

    # ── and one node this level does not want ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # ⚠ Off, and off on purpose rather than absent. `!Outline` finds depth and normal discontinuities
    # and draws them, which is a *look* — over a physically lit arena it reads as a rendering fault
    # rather than as a style, and over a selection mask it is the editor's highlight rather than a
    # game's. It is named here so the shape of a document that wants it is on the page: give it a
    # source, point `selectionMask` at what should be outlined, and turn it on.
    - !Outline
      name: Edging
      enabled: false
      source: SceneSharpened
      depth: SceneDepth
      normals: SceneNormals
      output: SceneOutlined
      useNormals: true
      selectionOnly: false
      colour: 1.0 0.6 0.0 1.0
      thickness: 1.5
      nearPlane: 0.1
      farPlane: 1000.0
      depthThreshold: 0.02

# ── the squares artefact: found, and what this note used to suspect ────────────────────────────────
# Small squares laid edge to edge, on ground near a lamp, gone after a second of standing still.
# The audit that closed this note found the cause upstream of every suspect it listed: the screen
# passes decoded the G-buffer's *raw signed* normals as if they were unorm (`* 2 − 1`), which tilted
# every flat surface's normal onto a fixed world diagonal — and the probe upsample's plane test then
# rejected every cross-tile tap on open floors, collapsing irradiance to one probe per 16-pixel
# tile. Sixteen-pixel squares, worst near a lamp where irradiance gradients are steep, converging
# when the accumulator catches up on a still camera. The decode is fixed at every consumer
# (Ssao, DistanceFieldAo, ScreenProbeUpsample, IndirectDiffuse, Outline, and the CPU placement).
#
# Of the old suspects: the virtualized pair is now disabled outright (see the node for the three
# reasons and the re-enable checklist); `!LocalExposure`'s twelve taps and `!TemporalAntialiasing`'s
# variance clipping remain the things to bisect first if a *new* tiled artefact ever appears, in
# that order.
